<article class="env-lemma" id="08G8">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08G8">Lemma <span data-tag="08G8">21.47.4</span>.</a> Let $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$ be a ringed site. Let $E$ be an object of $D(\mathcal{O})$. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$E$ is perfect, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$E$ is pseudo-coherent and locally has finite tor dimension. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume (1). Let $U$ be an object of $\mathcal{C}$. By definition there exists a covering $\{ U_ i \to U\} $ such that $E|_{U_ i}$ is represented by a strictly perfect complex. Thus $E$ is pseudo-coherent (i.e., $m$-pseudo-coherent for all $m$) by Lemma <a href="/tag/08FU" data-tag="08FU">21.45.2</a>. Moreover, a direct summand of a finite free module is flat, hence $E|_{U_ i}$ has finite Tor dimension by Lemma <a href="/tag/08G1" data-tag="08G1">21.46.3</a>. Thus (2) holds. 
    <p>
      Assume (2). Let $U$ be an object of $\mathcal{C}$. After replacing $U$ by the members of a covering we may assume there exist integers $a \leq b$ such that $E|_ U$ has tor amplitude in $[a, b]$. Since $E|_ U$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent for all $m$ we conclude using Lemma <a href="/tag/08G7" data-tag="08G7">21.47.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>